    answer: "Direct answer: a Washington DC rowhouse is underpinned by excavating small sequenced pits beneath its existing footings and pouring new concrete footings deeper to reach stable soil, or by installing helical piers or push piers down to a load-bearing stratum. The method is chosen from a geotechnical investigation of the soils under that specific block, because DC soils vary dramatically - from dense material on the higher ground to soft, compressible soils near buried stream valleys like the old Tiber Creek. The engineer designs the underpinning for the actual bearing stratum and the actual loads of that rowhouse, then the work is permitted through the District's building department with structural observation during construction.\n\nMost DC rowhouses were built between the 1870s and the 1920s with shallow footings: brick or rubble-stone walls that bottom out only a few feet below grade, sized for the light loads of the original construction. A century later, those footings may sit on soils that have consolidated, been undermined by old utility trenches, or softened by chronic water infiltration. The signs are familiar to anyone who owns one of these houses: stair-step cracks in the brick, sloping floors, doors that no longer latch, and a visible step where one party wall has moved relative to its neighbor. Underpinning does not lift the house back to level - it stops the movement by transferring the load to soil that will not move.\n\nPit underpinning, the traditional method, works one small section at a time. The contractor excavates a pit roughly three to four feet wide beneath the footing, leaving the adjacent footing fully supported, then pours concrete in the pit up to the underside of the existing footing. Only after that section has cured does the crew move to the next pit, leapfrogging along the wall so no more than a small fraction of the foundation is ever unsupported at once. The engineer specifies the pit width, the sequencing, and the concrete strength, and requires the new concrete to be dry-packed or grouted tight against the old footing so load transfers immediately. Where soils are too poor for even deepened footings, the design switches to piers: helical piers screwed into the ground to a verified torque, or push piers driven by the weight of the structure itself, each load-tested to confirm capacity.\n\nParty walls make DC underpinning a neighbor affair. Rowhouses share structural walls, so underpinning one house changes the support conditions of the houses on both sides, and DC practice requires notifying adjacent owners and often monitoring their walls for movement during the work. In the city's historic districts - Capitol Hill, Georgetown, LeDroit Park, and dozens of others - exterior work visible from the street also passes through historic preservation review, which runs on its own timeline alongside the building permit. The authority having jurisdiction decides what gets approved, so the engineer confirms both the building permit path and any preservation review before the geotechnical work begins.",
