        body: "Towers deserve their own assessment focus because they combine every structural risk in one element: great height, heavy masonry, wind exposure, and often a timber-framed steeple or belfry at the top. The engineer checks the tower for plumb, evaluates the masonry for the cumulative effects of a century of wind cycling, and inspects the belfry framing that carries the bells - dynamic loads from swinging bells stress connections that were never engineered for them. Open or missing louvers, failed flashing at the steeple base, and water entry at the belfry are the maintenance failures that turn into structural failures.\n\nRoofs are the other priority because a failed church roof destroys everything below it. The assessment traces interior plaster damage and masonry staining back to their roofing source, evaluates the truss and deck structure for the loads it actually carries - including mechanical equipment added over the decades - and specifies repairs that respect the historic framing. Where conditions are stable but worth watching, the engineer establishes a monitoring program: crack gauges, plumb measurements, or periodic re-surveys that catch movement early. Monitoring turns an uncertain condition into a managed one, and it gives the congregation time to plan and fund the eventual repair.",
