        heading: "Ties, Underpinning, and Neighbor Coordination",
        body: "Stitching is the workhorse repair for cracked but fundamentally sound walls. Stainless helical ties are grouted into drilled holes across cracks and between separated wythes, restoring the wall's ability to act as a unit without the disruption of rebuilding. Where brick is crushed or mortar is gone beyond repointing depth, the engineer specifies partial rebuilding - removing and relaying the failed zone in matched brick and lime mortar, toothed into the sound wall each side. Bulges that exceed stability limits are taken down and rebuilt; there is no tie strong enough to straighten a wall that has lost its geometry.\n\nUnderpinning addresses the foundation when settlement is the cause. The work proceeds in short sequenced sections - typically four to five feet - excavating beneath the existing footing, pouring new concrete to a competent bearing stratum, and packing the joint before moving to the next section, so the wall above is continuously supported. Throughout, the engineer coordinates access, shoring, and temporary weather protection with both owners, because the work zone straddles the property line by definition. Vibration and dust monitoring protect both buildings, and the repair drawings note the party-wall agreement as a permit condition.",
