    answer: "The Mid-Atlantic sits on geology that breathes radon - uranium-bearing rock and soils across Pennsylvania, Maryland, and Virginia feed the colorless gas into homes through every foundation crack. Direct answer: the engineer tests indoor radon levels, designs a sub-slab depressurization system with a sealed suction pit and a continuously vented fan, seals foundation entry routes, and verifies levels after installation, with local building permits in most jurisdictions and disclosure rules when the home sells. Mitigation is a pressure-management problem, not a filter.\n\nThe core of the design is sub-slab depressurization. A suction pit is cored through the basement slab, and a fan draws soil gas from beneath the slab and vents it above the roofline, reversing the pressure difference that pulls radon indoors. The engineer sizes the fan and pipe for the slab area and sub-slab material - gravel beds communicate suction widely, while tight clay soils may need multiple suction points. Discharge is routed to avoid re-entrainment through windows or intakes, and the system includes a manometer or monitor so the homeowner can confirm it is running. Sealing sump pits, cracks, and utility penetrations with urethane caulk and covers improves the system's reach.\n\nTesting brackets the whole project. A short-term or long-term test establishes the baseline before design, and a post-installation test - run with the system operating under closed-house conditions - confirms the reduction. The design target follows the federal action level guidance, but the engineering goal is as low as reasonably achievable, since no threshold is risk-free. In new construction, the engineer can specify a passive rough-in - a capped suction pit and vent stack routed through the house - so a future fan installation is a one-day job instead of a retrofit.\n\nPermits and rules vary by state and locality. Pennsylvania, Maryland, and Virginia each run their own radon programs with different testing and disclosure requirements at real estate transfer, and most local jurisdictions require a building or mechanical permit for the fan and electrical work. Some counties in the region's high-radon zones see mitigation as routine; elsewhere it is triggered by a test result during a sale. The engineer designs to the applicable state guidance and the local AHJ's permit requirements, and the test result after installation is the only approval that matters for performance.",
